      "title": "The Mold That Saved Millions",
      "summary": "Alexander Fleming studied virulent bacteria in his cluttered London hospital laboratory. A stray penicillium mold spore contaminated an open culture dish during summer vacation. The mold produced a powerful antibacterial agent, launching the antibiotic revolution.",
      "content": "A century ago, a single rose thorn or a minor paper cut could end your life. Harmful bacteria invaded the smallest scratch, multiplied rapidly, and overwhelmed the human body within days.\n\nDoctors watched helplessly as fatal fevers spread through packed hospital wards. They washed wounds with harsh chemicals and offered soothing words, but microscopic invaders usually won the fight.\n\nThat brutal reality changed on a morning in September 1928 at St. Mary’s Hospital in London. Alexander Fleming, a Scottish bacteriologist, had just returned to his laboratory from a summer holiday.\n\nFleming was a brilliant researcher, but his workbench was notoriously messy. Before leaving on vacation, he had stacked dozens of glass Petri dishes filled with deadly *Staphylococcus* bacteria in a corner.\n\nInstead of dumping the contaminated dishes into disinfectant, Fleming inspected them one by one. As he lifted a forgotten plate near an open window, something unusual caught his eye.\n\nA stray mold spore had landed on the nutrient jelly and sprouted into a fuzzy green colony. Around this fungal patch, the deadly bacteria had dissolved completely into clear fluid droplets.\n\nFleming stared closely through his lens and muttered, \"That's funny.\" He realized that the mold was producing a defensive chemical capable of destroying dangerous germs without harming human tissue.\n\nHe identified the invader as a rare *Penicillium* mold and named the active substance penicillin. However, extracting and purifying the delicate chemical proved almost impossible with his simple equipment.\n\nFor a full decade, penicillin remained an intriguing footnote in medical journals. Europe was sliding toward the Second World War, and soldiers continued to die from infected wounds.\n\nIn 1939, two scientists at Oxford University, Howard Florey and Ernst Chain, revived Fleming's forgotten discovery. They built makeshift factory equipment out of dairy bottles, metal bathtubs, and milk churns to grow the mold in bulk.\n\nTheir purified extract worked like magic, rescuing critically ill patients from deadly blood poisoning. American factories soon adopted industrial fermentation tanks, producing millions of doses just in time for Allied troops.\n\nPenicillin transformed modern medicine forever. A cluttered desk and a keen eye launched the antibiotic age, turning once-lethal infections into routine, treatable conditions."
