      "kind": "chapter",
      "prefix": "Chapter 2",
      "title": "The First Vaccine",
      "summary": "Smallpox raged through crowded eighteenth-century towns as a lethal scourge. Edward Jenner investigated countryside lore that milkmaids were immune after cowpox. His bold inoculation of young James Phipps founded the science of vaccination.",
      "content": "In the eighteenth century, smallpox was the most terrifying killer on Earth. The airborne virus swept through crowded cities, killing one out of every three victims and leaving survivors blinded or scarred for life.\n\nDoctors were completely powerless against the recurring epidemics. Parents prayed desperately over feverish children, while authorities burned contaminated clothing in hopeless attempts to halt the contagion.\n\nDeep in the English countryside, a rural doctor named Edward Jenner listened closely to local farmers. Dairymaids were famous throughout Gloucestershire for their remarkably smooth, flawless skin.\n\nCountry folklore claimed that dairymaids never caught smallpox because they regularly contracted cowpox from the animals. Cowpox was a mild infection that produced small sores on milkers' hands, but faded away harmlessly within weeks.\n\nJenner wondered whether surviving the mild cattle disease trained the human body to resist the deadly smallpox virus. If the rumor proved true, doctors could protect people without risking their lives.\n\nIn May 1796, Jenner risked his medical reputation on a daring experiment. He met Sarah Nelmes, a young milkmaid suffering from active cowpox blisters on her hands.\n\nJenner scraped fluid from Sarah's blister with a needle. He then made two small scratches on the arm of James Phipps, his gardener’s eight-year-old son, and applied the fluid.\n\nYoung James developed a mild fever and minor discomfort, but he bounced back quickly. Several weeks later, Jenner prepared the critical, terrifying test that would shock modern medical boards.\n\nJenner intentionally inoculated the young boy with virulent matter taken directly from a smallpox victim. For days, Jenner watched anxiously by the bedside, knowing that any mistake meant death.\n\nThe gamble paid off completely: James remained energetic, healthy, and entirely free of infection. Jenner repeated the bold procedure on several other volunteers, including his own baby son, achieving the same miraculous protection.\n\nJenner called the process *vaccination*, derived from *vacca*, the Latin word for cow. Skeptics in London initially ridiculed the breakthrough, publishing cartoons of patients sprouting cow horns and snouts.\n\nYet the lifesaving results could not be denied, and vaccination soon spread across the globe. By 1980, the World Health Organization declared that Jenner's bold idea had completely eradicated smallpox from the planet."
