      "id": "part_02-ch_06",
      "position": 8,
      "kind": "chapter",
      "prefix": "Chapter 6",
      "title": "Bread from the Air",
      "summary": "Depleted soils and agricultural limits threatened worldwide famine by 1900. Fritz Haber developed high-pressure chemical synthesis to fix atmospheric nitrogen. Synthetic ammonia fertilizer expanded global food yields and sustained billions.",
      "content": "In 1898, British scientist William Crookes issued a chilling warning to the Royal Society. Unless chemists figured out how to extract fertilizer directly from the air, the world was doomed to starve.\n\nGlobal population had surged past 1.6 billion, and farmlands were completely exhausted. Crops desperately needed nitrogen to grow, but intense farming had drained the soil dry.\n\nTo keep famine at bay, nations fought over the only known reserves: ancient bird droppings and mineral saltpeter mined from Chilean deserts.\n\nThese precious supplies were vanishing fast. Within decades, steamships would arrive in Chile only to find empty quarries and barren cliffs.\n\nThe great irony was that humanity was literally drowning in nitrogen. The gas makes up nearly eighty percent of the atmosphere we breathe every day.\n\nYet plants cannot absorb pure nitrogen gas. Its two atoms are locked together by an unbreakable chemical bond, drifting uselessly through the sky.\n\nIn Karlsruhe, Germany, a chemist named Fritz Haber decided to force those stubborn atoms apart. His goal was to combine nitrogen with hydrogen to create ammonia, the foundation of modern fertilizer.\n\nColleagues warned Haber that the project was suicidal. Breaking the nitrogen bond required blistering temperatures and crushing pressure, a recipe for violent industrial explosions.\n\nInside his laboratory, Haber built a reinforced steel reaction chamber. He packed the cylinder with exotic metal catalysts like osmium and uranium, heating the core to over five hundred degrees Celsius.\n\nNext, he pumped in compressed gases at two hundred times normal atmospheric pressure. One tiny crack in the pipework would blow the building apart.\n\nOn July 2, 1909, the machine began to hum under immense strain. Haber and his assistant stared anxiously at a small glass observation tube.\n\nSuddenly, a clear drop rolled down the inside of the glass. Then came another, and another—a steady trickle of pure liquid ammonia.\n\nHaber had conquered the atmosphere, but building a benchtop experiment was one thing. Scaling it into an industrial giant was another monster entirely.\n\nEnter Carl Bosch, a brilliant metallurgical engineer with nerves of steel. Bosch spent years designing giant chemical reactors that could handle hot, corrosive hydrogen without rupturing.\n\nBy 1913, the world’s first industrial ammonia plant was fully operational. The Haber-Bosch process instantly reshaped human existence.\n\nSynthetic nitrogen fertilizers multiplied crop harvests across the globe, allowing humanity to produce unprecedented amounts of food.\n\nToday, nearly four billion people owe their very lives to this single reaction. Look at any loaf of bread: the nitrogen inside it was literally pulled from thin air."
