      "title": "The Energy Inside the Atom",
      "summary": "Nineteenth-century physicists regarded indivisible atoms as inert, immutable matter. Marie Curie isolated polonium and radium from heavy pitchblende in a drafty shed. Her discovery proved atoms emit vast subatomic energy, redefining physical science.",
      "content": "In the late nineteenth century, physicists believed they had unraveled nature's deepest secrets.\n\nAtoms were assumed to be indestructible, solid billiard balls that had existed unchanged since the beginning of time.\n\nThe law of conservation of energy seemed indisputable. Inert matter could never release continuous energy without an external fuel source.\n\nThen came a perplexing puzzle. French scientist Henri Becquerel discovered that uranium salts blackened sealed photographic plates, even when locked away in complete darkness.\n\nWhile most researchers viewed this as a minor curiosity, a brilliant Polish-born scientist in Paris saw something profound. Her name was Maria Skłodowska Curie.\n\nWorking in a drafty, abandoned wooden shed, Marie and her husband Pierre built a sensitive electrometer to measure the faint electrical charge radiating from minerals.\n\nWhen Marie tested pitchblende, a heavy black uranium ore, the instrument registered shocking numbers. The raw ore was far more active than pure uranium itself.\n\nMarie drew an audacious conclusion. Pitchblende had to conceal a brand-new, ultra-powerful chemical element hidden in microscopic quantities.\n\nProving her theory required years of punishing physical toil. The couple ordered tons of pitchblende waste from Austrian mines, hauling heavy sacks into their freezing workshop.\n\nMarie spent long days stirring boiling cauldrons with heavy iron rods, inhaling toxic chemical fumes through summer heat and winter chills.\n\nHer perseverance conquered the impossible. In 1898, she isolated a new element and named it polonium to honor her beloved homeland of Poland.\n\nSoon after, the Curies isolated radium, a substance millions of times more potent than uranium.\n\nIn the dark shed, test tubes of radium glowed with an eerie blue-green light, constantly releasing heat without any chemical burning.\n\nMarie coined the term *radioactivity* to describe this spontaneous outpouring of subatomic power.\n\nHer discovery shattered classical physics. Atoms were not inert, permanent particles; they were dynamic, unstable structures capable of releasing unimaginable energy.\n\nMarie Curie became the first person to win two Nobel Prizes, in Physics and Chemistry. Her courage unlocked the atomic age, transforming medicine, energy, and human understanding forever."
