      "prefix": "Chapter 17",
      "title": "Letters of Lead",
      "summary": "Medieval scribes copied manuscripts by hand, keeping books scarce and costly. Johannes Gutenberg crafted durable movable metal type, reusable matrices, and oil-based ink. The printing press democratized literacy, science, and the spread of new ideas.",
      "content": "In the fifteenth century, a single book could cost as much as an entire farm.\n\nEvery page had to be copied by hand. Monks spent months sitting in freezing stone rooms, carefully tracing Latin words onto animal skin with feather quills.\n\nBecause of this slow process, Europe owned very few books. Royal courts and wealthy cathedrals chained their precious volumes to heavy desks to prevent theft.\n\nFor ordinary people, books did not exist. Knowledge stayed locked away in the hands of kings and priests, and new ideas traveled at a painful crawl.\n\nJohannes Gutenberg wanted to smash that barrier. In the German city of Mainz, the ambitious goldsmith spent years working on a secretive, expensive project.\n\nGutenberg knew that traditional wood carving was a dead end. Carving letters into wooden blocks took too long, and soft wood cracked under heavy pressure.\n\nInstead, Gutenberg turned to metal. He carved crisp letters onto the ends of hard steel punches and hammered them into softer copper bars.\n\nThese copper marks became precise molds. Gutenberg poured a melted blend of lead, tin, and antimony into the molds, creating identical metal letters by the thousands.\n\nThese tiny blocks of type were durable, uniform, and completely reusable. A printer could arrange them into words, print a page, and quickly sort them to build another.\n\nNext, Gutenberg solved the ink problem. Standard water-based ink slipped off metal letters and ruined the paper with dark smudges.\n\nHe mixed linseed oil with soot to produce a thick, sticky paste. The rich black ink gripped the metal letters cleanly and transferred sharp lines onto the page.\n\nTo apply steady pressure, Gutenberg modified a heavy wooden screw press used in local vineyards. With one pull of a wooden lever, a worker pressed an entire page in seconds.\n\nAround 1455, Gutenberg unveiled his triumph: the magnificent forty-two-line Latin Bible. Visitors gasped at the clean columns and sharp letters, wondering if an angel had crafted it.\n\nThe new technology spread across Europe like wildfire. Within fifty years, printing workshops operated in over two hundred cities, producing twenty million books.\n\nGutenberg’s letters of metal broke the monopoly on knowledge. Suddenly, fresh scientific theories, medical guides, and revolutionary religious debates reached thousands of eager readers in weeks."
